Id: lin049w
Severity: WARN
Category: kernel
Check: check_sysctl
Fix: Set kernel.ctrl-alt-del=0 in /etc/sysctl.d.
References: Linux kernel sysctl documentation (admin-guide/sysctl/kernel, ctrl-alt-del); systemd.special(7)
Controls: CIS 4.1, 10.5; NIST CM-6, SI-16; ISO 8.9; CE Secure configuration

kernel.ctrl-alt-del is above 0: Ctrl-Alt-Del on the console reboots
the machine at once, without syncing the disks, so what was written
last is lost and the file systems come up dirty. With 0, the kernel's
default on every distribution, the key is handed to init: systemd
starts ctrl-alt-del.target, an orderly reboot, and 'systemctl mask
ctrl-alt-del.target' turns even that off where console users are not
to reboot the host. Nothing ships a value above 0: it was set here.
  # sysctl -w kernel.ctrl-alt-del=0
